.\" Template for dedupctl.1: scripts/gen-docs.py fills in the marked parts
.\" from the dispatcher, libexec/dedupctl/*.sh and lib/*.sh, reading them only.
.TH DEDUPCTL 1 2026-11-08 "dedupctl" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dedupctl \- encrypted, compressed BorgBackup archives of a directory tree
.SH SYNOPSIS
.\" @generated synopsis
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dedupctl
keeps one Borg repository per backed-up directory.
.B init
marks a directory as a backup source by writing a
.I .borgbackup
file into it, see
.BR dedupctl.borgbackup (5),
and creates the repository under
.BR $BORG_BACKUP_ROOT ,
named after the directory's absolute path with every
.B /
turned into
.B !
(and every
.B !
doubled), encrypted with
.BR repokey .
.PP
Every other command looks for the nearest
.I .borgbackup
in the current directory or its parents, changes into that directory,
and reads the file.
A command that needs an archive and is given none lists the archives and
asks for one by number.
.PP
Arguments are read only after the command; the dispatcher itself knows
only
.BR \-h " and " \-\-help .
Every command refuses an option it does not know with status 2 before it
does anything, and every command takes
.BR \-h " and " \-\-help .
.SH OPTIONS
.\" @generated options
.SH COMMANDS
.\" @generated commands
.\" @prose backup
.PP
The archive is named after the time it starts,
.IR YYYY\-MM\-DD_HH:MM:SS ,
and is written with
.B borg create \-\-compression lz4
from
.BR SOURCE_DIR ,
leaving out the
.B EXCLUDES
of
.IR .borgbackup .
Its output also goes to
.IR $BORG_REPO/logs/<time>.log .
.B \-\-dry\-run
is passed to
.B borg create
and writes no log, so the repository is left as it was.
.PP
When a directory above the repository root has a
.I .borgbackup
of its own, the backup then runs there too, with the same options: a
.B \-\-dry\-run
stays a dry run in the parent.
.\" @end
.\" @prose prune
.PP
Each archive is removed with
.BR "borg delete" .
In interactive mode the archives are listed and the numbers to remove
are read, or
.BR all ;
both modes ask for confirmation first.
.\" @end
.\" @prose diff
.PP
Two archives are compared with
.BR "borg diff" .
The live state is compared by extracting the archive into a temporary
directory, removed afterwards, and running
.B diff \-r
against it.
.\" @end
.\" @prose log
.PP
The log is shown with
.BR less (1).
.\" @end
.\" @prose mount
.PP
The mount point is created when missing.
Unmount with
.B borg umount
.I mount_point
or
.BR umount (8).
.\" @end
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, also
.BR \-h " / " \-\-help
after any command or none, and
.B init
on a directory that already has a
.IR .borgbackup .
.TP
.B 1
No command or an unknown command (the usage text goes to standard
error), no
.I .borgbackup
was found, a selection was invalid, or another error.
A failing
.B borg
command ends the run with its own status.
.TP
.B 2
An option the command does not know,
.B init
given an option, more than one path or a path that is not a directory,
or
.B prune
given more than one of its options, or one without its number.
.TP
.B 3
.I .borgbackup
is missing or does not set a variable the command needs.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BORG_BACKUP_ROOT
Where
.B init
creates repositories; default
.IR ~/.borg_backups .
.TP
.BR BORG_PASSPHRASE ", " BORG_PASSCOMMAND ", ..."
Read by
.B borg
itself, which every command runs; see
.BR borg (1).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I .borgbackup
The backup source's configuration, in the directory it backs up; see
.BR dedupctl.borgbackup (5).
.TP
.I $BORG_BACKUP_ROOT/<name>/
The repository
.B init
creates.
.TP
.I $BORG_REPO/logs/*.log
One log per backup, read by
.BR log .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dedupctl.borgbackup (5),
.BR borg (1),
.BR hlinkctl (1)
